import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="How I Rate Gear | Nether"
  description="How I assess products and arrive at gear scores for Nether reviews."
>
  <main class="archive-page rating-method-page">
    <header class="archive-header">
      <a class="post-return" href="/gear/">
        ← Gear
      </a>
      <h1>How I rate gear</h1>
      <p>
        I score products against the things that matter most in their category,
        then weigh those observations against the price and the alternatives.
        I’m clear when a score is based on specifications and reviewer consensus
        rather than my own time with the product.
      </p>
    </header>

    <ol class="rating-criteria">
      <li>
        <h2>Keyboards</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Build quality:</strong> I look at the case, plate, stabilizers,
          keycaps, assembly, and any signs of flex or rattle. A board should feel
          dependable on the desk, not just look good in a product photo.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Typing feel:</strong> I pay attention to switch consistency,
          stabilizer tuning, mount, and how the whole board responds while I
          type. Those details matter more to the daily experience than a switch
          name on its own.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Sound:</strong> I listen for harsh resonance, rattling, and
          distracting inconsistency, while keeping in mind that keycaps,
          switches, the desk, and recording conditions all change what I hear.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Features and value:</strong> I consider layout, connectivity,
          remapping, hot-swap support, and useful extras against the asking
          price. A feature only helps the score when it works well and suits the
          people the board is meant for.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h2>Mice</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Shape and ergonomics:</strong> I judge whether the mouse feels
          natural for its intended grip and hand size, and whether its shape,
          buttons, and scroll wheel stay comfortable through a long session.
          Fit is personal, so I explain who is most likely to get along with it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Sensor and weight:</strong> I check tracking consistency,
          lift-off behavior, responsiveness, and whether the weight suits the
          mouse’s purpose. A low number on the spec sheet is not automatically
          better if it makes the mouse less comfortable or controlled.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Build and controls:</strong> I look for reliable clicks,
          steady buttons, a useful wheel, and materials that hold up to regular
          handling. For wireless models, I also consider connectivity and
          battery details.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Value:</strong> I compare comfort and performance with
          similarly priced mice. Extra buttons or software options count when
          they solve a real problem, not simply because they are present.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h2>IEMs</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Tuning:</strong> I assess the balance of bass, mids, and
          treble, and describe who might enjoy that presentation. Frequency
          graphs can help explain a tuning, but they do not tell me exactly how
          a set will sound or fit for every listener.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Technical performance:</strong> I listen for separation,
          dynamics, perceived detail, and space, while avoiding the assumption
          that a driver count or a sharp treble peak equals better sound.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Comfort and fit:</strong> I consider shell shape, nozzle,
          weight, tip options, seal, and isolation. A poor seal can change both
          comfort and bass, so fit is part of the sound assessment too.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Build and value:</strong> I check the shell, connectors, cable,
          and included accessories, then compare the complete package with
          alternatives at the same price.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h2>How I test</h2>
        <p>
          For a hands-on score, I use a product in ordinary daily work or
          listening for at least two weeks, then revisit the details that are
          easy to miss in a quick first impression. I compare it directly with
          other products in the same category and price range, and note the
          conditions that could affect the result.
        </p>
        <p>
          When I have not personally tested something, I label the score as
          specification- and consensus-based rather than presenting it as my
          own hands-on experience.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h2>What the score means</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>6/10</strong> means there are worthwhile qualities, but the
          compromises are noticeable or better options exist at a similar
          price. <strong>8/10</strong> means I recommend it for the right person
          and its trade-offs are reasonable. <strong>10/10</strong> is rare: it
          means I can find no meaningful weakness for its intended use and
          price, not that it is perfect for everyone.
        </p>
        <p>
          These ratings are my opinion, based on the information and testing I
          can support. Your preferences, fit, and priorities may lead you to a
          different score.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </main>
</SiteLayout>
